{{ m.sourceNote("ifs-rise-without-tl" | figure) }}

<h4 id="saving-2030">The rule announced for April 2030</h4>

<p>The government estimates a saving of {{ m.f("saving-rule-2030-late-2030s") }} a year by the end of the 2030s, and {{ m.f("saving-rule-2030-2050") }} a year by 2050.</p>
{{ m.sourceNote("saving-rule-2030-2050" | figure) }}

<p>{{ ("ifs-savings-small-early" | claim).statement }} {{ m.c("ifs-savings-small-early") }} To show the scale, the Institute looks back. If the announced rule had applied since 2011, spending in 2026-27 would be {{ m.f("rule-2030-counterfactual-ifs-2026") }} lower. That is a look back, not a forecast.</p>
{{ m.sourceNote("rule-2030-counterfactual-ifs-2026" | figure) }}

{% call m.note("Why the government's figures cannot yet be checked", 5) %}
<p>The government's figure for 2050 is higher than the top of the Institute for Fiscal Studies' range, above, for what the triple lock itself would cost in 2050. That range is in the prices of 2025. The government does not give the prices its figures are in, so the two may not be on the same basis. {{ ("no-rule-2030-detail" | claim).statement }} {{ m.c("no-rule-2030-detail") }}</p>
{% endcall %}

{% call m.unavailable("costings for a double lock of prices or earnings, and for means-testing", 4) %}
<p>{{ ("no-costed-double-lock" | claim).statement }} {{ ("no-costed-means-test" | claim).statement }} We have not made our own estimates. A calculation that multiplied one person's difference by the number of pensioners would be wrong, because people receive different amounts and because tax and benefits change the result.</p>
{% endcall %}

<p>To see the State Pension beside other spending and taxes, the {{ m.sisterLink("budget-simulator" | link) }} lets you change each one. It is a simplified teaching model, not an official costing of any pension reform, and its State Pension figure is a forecast for an earlier year than the figures on this page. To put a figure in billions into scale, there is the {{ m.sisterLink("big-numbers" | link) }}. An amount per person from that tool is simple division. It is not what anyone pays or receives.</p>

<h2 id="who">Who receives what: incomes, wealth and poverty</h2>

<p>An average pensioner does not exist. The evidence below is about the spread.</p>

<h3>Income</h3>

{{ m.dataTable("income-fifths") }}

<p>The State Pension matters most to people with the lowest incomes, and it still matters to those with the highest. It makes up {{ m.f("sp-share-income-poorest") }} of income for the poorest fifth of pensioners and {{ m.f("sp-share-income-richest") }} for the richest fifth.</p>
{{ m.sourceNote("sp-share-income-poorest" | figure) }}

<p>On this page, "low income" and "high income" mean a position in these distributions, unless a source's own definition is given.</p>

<h3>Poverty and deprivation</h3>

<ul class="cards cards--three">
  <li>{{ m.stat("low-income-ahc" | figure, "After rent, mortgage interest and similar costs are taken off.", 4) }}</li>
  <li>{{ m.stat("low-income-bhc" | figure, "Before housing costs are taken off.", 4) }}</li>
  <li>{{ m.stat("material-deprivation" | figure, "A measure of going without, not of income.", 4) }}</li>
</ul>

<p>Fewer pensioners are in low income after housing costs than before, because most own their home outright. {{ m.c("hbai-owners") }} For a pensioner who rents, housing costs work the other way.</p>

<h3>Housing and wealth</h3>

<p>Of households headed by someone aged 65 or over, {{ m.f("tenure-own-outright") }} own outright, {{ m.f("tenure-mortgage") }} are buying with a mortgage, {{ m.f("tenure-social-rent") }} rent from a social landlord and {{ m.f("tenure-private-rent") }} rent privately.</p>
{{ m.sourceNote("tenure-own-outright" | figure) }}

{{ m.dataTable("wealth-bands") }}

<p>Wealth is spread more unevenly than income. Among single households over State Pension age, a quarter have total wealth below {{ m.f("wealth-single-p25") }} and a quarter have more than {{ m.f("wealth-single-p75") }}.</p>
{{ m.sourceNote("wealth-single-p25" | figure) }}

{% call m.note("Income, wealth and need are three different things", 4) %}
<p>A person can have a valuable home and a low income. A home cannot pay a bill unless it is sold or borrowed against. A person can have a high income and high costs, for example for care. People with high income and people with high wealth are not the same group, and neither measure shows what a person needs.</p>
{% endcall %}

<h3>Differences between men and women</h3>

<p>Among people aged 65 to 74, the median private pension wealth of men is {{ m.f("pension-wealth-men-65-74") }} and of women is {{ m.f("pension-wealth-women-65-74") }}, counting people with none. Women on the older State Pension system also receive less on average than men. <a href="/state-pension/#what-people-receive">What people actually receive</a>. A person with little private pension depends more on the State Pension, and so is more exposed to a change in it.</p>
{{ m.sourceNote("pension-wealth-women-65-74" | figure) }}

<h3>Disability and caring</h3>

<p>{{ m.f("disabled-share-spa") }} of adults of State Pension age report a disability. {{ ("disability-income-artefact" | claim).statement }} {{ m.c("disability-income-artefact") }}</p>
{{ m.sourceNote("disabled-share-spa" | figure) }}

{#- Level 3 on purpose. This covers the whole section on who receives what, not the subsection above it. -#}
{% call m.unavailable("some breakdowns", 3) %}
<p>We did not find published figures for the incomes of pensioners who are carers, for wealth by type among people aged 65 and over, or for the number of people who would gain or lose under each reform. We have not filled these gaps with examples. Example households can show how a rule works. They cannot show how many people are affected.</p>
{% endcall %}

<h2 id="increases">Options for the yearly increase</h2>

<p>The page describes each option the same way, including the rule the government announced on {{ ("pm-care-2026-09-29" | sourceRecord).published_date | timeTag | safe }}. The law sets a minimum of earnings for any option. A rule that pays less than earnings growth would need a change in the law. {{ m.c("legal-earnings-floor") }}</p>

<article class="option">
  <h3 id="opt-2030">The rule announced for April 2030</h3>
  <dl>
    <dt>The rule</dt> <dd>{{ ("rule-2030" | claim).statement }} {{ m.c("rule-2030") }} {{ ("ifs-rule-2030-mechanism" | claim).statement }} {{ m.c("ifs-rule-2030-mechanism") }} {{ ("rule-2030-name" | claim).statement }} {{ ("press-double-lock" | claim).statement }} {{ m.c("press-double-lock") }} It is not the double lock described on this page.</dd>
    <dt>Effect on the pension</dt> <dd>{{ ("rule-2030-no-fall" | claim).statement }} {{ m.c("rule-2030-no-fall") }} {{ ("ifs-rule-2030-catch-up" | claim).statement }} {{ ("ifs-ratchet-removed" | claim).statement }} {{ m.c("ifs-ratchet-removed") }} {{ ("ifs-real-rise-slower" | claim).statement }} {{ m.c("ifs-real-rise-slower") }} Looking back, the Institute says the pension would still have risen {{ m.f("rule-2030-real-rise-ifs") }} faster than prices from 2010 to 2026 under the rule. {{ m.cite(("rule-2030-real-rise-ifs" | figure).source) }}. The Resolution Foundation says that if the rule had applied since 2012 the pension this year would be {{ m.f("rule-2030-vs-tl-rf") }} lower than it is, and {{ m.f("rule-2030-vs-earnings-rf") }} higher than under an earnings link. {{ m.cite(("rule-2030-vs-tl-rf" | figure).source) }}.</dd>
    <dt>Who is affected</dt> <dd>Nobody before April 2030. {{ m.c("tl-kept-to-2030") }} After that, the same percentage would apply to every rate the rule covers. The announcement does not say whether it covers the same parts of a payment as the triple lock does now. {{ ("no-rule-2030-distribution" | claim).statement }} {{ ("no-rule-2030-pension-credit" | claim).statement }} {{ m.c("no-rule-2030-distribution") }}</dd>
    <dt>Public finances</dt> <dd>Small savings at first, growing over decades. <a href="#saving-2030">The estimates, and why they cannot yet be checked</a>.</dd>
    <dt>The law</dt> <dd>{{ ("rule-2030-legislate" | claim).statement }} Our reading: {{ ("rule-2030-needs-law" | claim).statement }} {{ m.c("rule-2030-needs-law") }} {{ ("no-rule-2030-detail" | claim).statement }}</dd>
  </dl>
</article>

<div class="option-grid">
  <article class="option">
    <h3 id="opt-triple">Keep the triple lock</h3>
    <dl>
      <dt>The rule</dt> <dd>The highest of prices, earnings and 2.5%.</dd>
      <dt>Effect on the pension</dt> <dd>It rises at least as fast as prices and as earnings, every year. Over time it rises faster than both. The level in future is uncertain. The Institute for Fiscal Studies gives <q>{{ ("ifs-range-2050" | claim).passage }}</q>. {{ m.c("ifs-range-2050") }}</dd>
      <dt>Who is affected</dt> <dd>Current pensioners, and future ones more so, because increases build up over time. {{ m.c("ppi-future-workers") }} People who live longer gain more. {{ m.c("ifs-longevity") }}</dd>
      <dt>Public finances</dt> <dd>The highest cost of the four rules, and the least predictable.</dd>
      <dt>A judgement, not a finding</dt> <dd>Whether the pension should rise as a share of earnings, and how far.</dd>
    </dl>
  </article>
  <article class="option">
    <h3 id="opt-double">Double lock: prices or earnings</h3>
    <dl>
      <dt>The rule</dt> <dd>The higher of prices and earnings. The 2.5% minimum is removed.</dd>
      <dt>Effect on the pension</dt> <dd>The same as the triple lock in any year when prices or earnings rise by 2.5% or more. Lower in other years. It still rises faster than both prices and earnings over time, because it takes the higher each year.</dd>
      <dt>Who is affected</dt> <dd>The same people, by less. Modelling from 2018 put the effect on pensioner poverty in 2050 at {{ "ppi-poverty-double-lock" | figShown }} more people than under the triple lock. {{ m.cite(("ppi-poverty-earnings-link" | figure).source) }}.</dd>
      <dt>Public finances</dt> <dd>{{ ("no-costed-double-lock" | claim).statement }}</dd>
      <dt>A precedent</dt> <dd>{{ ("double-lock-2017" | claim).statement }} {{ m.c("double-lock-2017") }}</dd>
    </dl>
  </article>
  <article class="option">
    <h3 id="opt-earnings">Earnings link</h3>
    <dl>
      <dt>The rule</dt> <dd>The rise in average earnings. This is the minimum the law already requires.</dd>
      <dt>Effect on the pension</dt> <dd>It keeps its value compared with pay. In a year when prices rise faster than pay, it loses buying power.</dd>
      <dt>Who is affected</dt> <dd>Modelling from 2018 put the number of extra pensioners in poverty in 2050 at {{ "ppi-poverty-earnings-link" | figShown }}, compared with the triple lock. {{ m.sourceNote("ppi-poverty-earnings-link" | figure) }}</dd>
      <dt>Public finances</dt> <dd>Small savings at first, large over decades. See the table above.</dd>
      <dt>A judgement, not a finding</dt> <dd>Whether a year of lost buying power is acceptable if the pension keeps pace with pay over time.</dd>
    </dl>
  </article>
  <article class="option">
    <h3 id="opt-prices">Prices link</h3>
    <dl>
      <dt>The rule</dt> <dd>The rise in the Consumer Prices Index.</dd>
      <dt>Effect on the pension</dt> <dd>It keeps its buying power against the official index. When pay rises faster than prices, which is the usual long-run pattern, it falls further behind pay each year.</dd>
      <dt>Who is affected</dt> <dd>Future pensioners most, because the gap with earnings grows with time.</dd>
      <dt>Public finances</dt> <dd>The largest saving of the four. See the table above.</dd>
      <dt>The law</dt> <dd>It would pay less than the present legal minimum in most years, so the law would have to change. From 1980 to 2010 the law required only a prices link. {{ m.c("earnings-link-1980") }}</dd>
    </dl>
  </article>
</div>

<h3 id="how-2030-works">How the announced rule works: an example</h3>

<p>{{ ("ifs-rule-2030-illustration" | claim).statement }} {{ m.c("ifs-rule-2030-illustration") }} {{ ("ifs-rule-2030-illustration" | claim).note }}</p>

<h3 id="smoothed">A smoothed earnings link, and why two rules are not in the calculator</h3>

<p>{{ ("smoothed-link-definition" | claim).statement }} {{ m.c("smoothed-link-definition") }} {{ ("ifs-smoothed-similar" | claim).statement }} {{ m.c("ifs-smoothed-similar") }} Before the announcement it was proposed, in different forms, by three research bodies.</p>
<ul>
  <li>{{ ("ifs-guarantee-1" | claim).statement }} {{ ("ifs-guarantee-2" | claim).statement }} {{ ("ifs-not-immediately" | claim).statement }} {{ m.c("ifs-guarantee-1") }}</li>
  <li>{{ ("rf-smoothed-recommendation" | claim).statement }} {{ m.c("rf-smoothed-recommendation") }}</li>
  <li>{{ ("tbi-proposal" | claim).statement }} {{ m.c("tbi-proposal") }}</li>
</ul>
<p>These are recommendations. They rest on evidence and on a view about what the pension should be worth.</p>

{% call m.note("Why these two rules are not in the calculator", 4) %}
<p>The proposals for a smoothed link differ in three details. They are the target level, how the pension catches up after a year of price protection, and over how long. For the rule announced for April 2030, the government has not published which measure of earnings would be used, or from what starting point. It gives an example only. {{ ("rule-2030-example" | claim).statement }} {{ m.c("rule-2030-example") }} Until those are specified and checked, a calculation would be our invention. Both rules are described here and left out of the comparison below.</p>
{% endcall %}

<p>A freeze, with no increase at all, is not among the options. We found no current proposal for one.</p>

<section class="tool" aria-labelledby="compare">
  <h2 id="compare">Try it: compare the rules</h2>

  <p>This tool applies four rules to the same starting amount and the same yearly figures for prices and earnings. It shows where the rules differ and where they do not. The rule announced for April 2030 is not one of the four. <a href="#smoothed">Why it is not in the calculator</a>.</p>

  {% call m.note("What this tool does and does not show", 3) %}
  <ul>
    <li>It models <strong>one full rate</strong>, weekly and <strong>before tax</strong>. It is not your entitlement.</li>
    <li>The yearly figures are <strong>illustrations</strong>. They are not forecasts and have no probability.</li>
    <li>It leaves out Income Tax, Pension Credit and every other benefit. A difference shown here is not a difference in money to spend.</li>
    <li>You cannot use it to work out national savings.</li>
    <li>The tool does not send or store anything you enter.</li>
  </ul>
  {% endcall %}

  <div class="no-js-only">
    {% call m.note("The calculator needs JavaScript", 3) %}
    <p>The form is not available, because JavaScript is off or did not load. The result below shows the first scenario.</p>
    {% endcall %}
  </div>

  <form id="compare-form" class="js-only" novalidate>
    <fieldset>
      <legend>Settings</legend>
      <div class="fields">
        <div class="field">
          <label for="compare-scenario">Scenario</label>
          <select id="compare-scenario" name="compare-scenario">
            {%- for item in scenarios.scenarios %}
            <option value="{{ item.id }}"{% if item.id == scenarios.default %} selected{% endif %}>{{ item.label }}</option>
            {%- endfor %}
          </select>
        </div>
        {{ m.field("compare-years", "Number of years", scenarios.default_years, "From 1 to 30.", "", "", "numeric") }}
        {{ m.field("compare-start", "Starting weekly amount", ("nsp-full-weekly" | figPence) | plainPounds, "A full rate, in pounds. The full new State Pension for 2026/27 is filled in.", "£", "") }}
        <div class="field">
          <label for="compare-first">First year under the rule</label>
          <span class="hint" id="compare-first-hint">This changes the year labels only. The starting amount is the rate for the year before.</span>
          <select id="compare-first" name="compare-first" aria-describedby="compare-first-hint">
            {%- for y in range(2027, 2037) %}
            <option value="{{ y }}">{{ y }}/{{ (y + 1) % 100 }}</option>
            {%- endfor %}
          </select>
        </div>
        <div class="field">
          <label for="compare-measure">Show amounts as</label>
          <select id="compare-measure" name="compare-measure">
            <option value="cash">Cash: the money of each year</option>
            <option value="real">Buying power: the prices of the starting year</option>
            <option value="earnings">Compared with earnings: an index</option>
          </select>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="choice">
        <input type="checkbox" id="compare-floor" name="compare-floor" checked aria-describedby="compare-floor-hint">
        <label for="compare-floor">The amount never falls in pounds (a cash floor of 0%)</label>
      </div>
      <p class="hint" id="compare-floor-hint">This is an assumption of these hypothetical rules. It is not a statement of the law. It matters only when prices or earnings fall.</p>
      <dl class="reason"><dt>Why this scenario is here</dt> <dd id="compare-reason">{{ scenario.reason }}</dd></dl>
    </fieldset>

    <details class="more" id="compare-edit">
      <summary>Change the figures for each year</summary>
      <p>Per cent, from -10 to 25, with one decimal place. All four rules use the same figures.</p>
      <div class="scroll-x" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="Figures for each year">
        <table>
          <caption>Rise in prices and earnings used for each year</caption>
          <thead><tr><th scope="col">Tax year</th><th scope="col">Rise in prices (%)</th><th scope="col">Rise in earnings (%)</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody id="compare-rows"></tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
    </details>

    <div class="buttons">
      <button type="submit">Show the result</button>
      <button type="button" class="secondary" id="compare-reset">Reset everything</button>
    </div>
  </form>

  <p class="visually-hidden" role="status" id="compare-status"></p>

  <div class="result" id="compare-result">
    {{ { startPence: ("nsp-full-weekly" | figPence), path: scenario.path.slice(0, scenarios.default_years), cashFloor: true, firstYear: 2027, measure: "cash", scenarioLabel: scenario.label | lower } | renderComparison | safe }}
  </div>

  <h3>The other scenarios</h3>
  <dl>
    {%- for item in scenarios.scenarios %}
    <dt>{{ item.label }}</dt>
    <dd>{{ item.reason }}{% if item.source_id %} {% set s = item.source_id | sourceRecord %}<span class="ref">Source for the two rates: {{ m.cite(s) }}.</span>{% endif %}</dd>
    {%- endfor %}
  </dl>
  <p>The scenarios were chosen to show different behaviour, including scenarios where several rules give the same answer. A result changes with the starting amount, the number of years and the figures. No single scenario shows what will happen.</p>

  <h3>How the tool calculates</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Timing.</strong> The starting amount is the rate in the year before the first year. Each year has one figure for prices and one for earnings, and they set that year's increase.</li>
    <li><strong>A simplification.</strong> In practice an April increase uses figures from the year before. The tool does not model that delay. It treats each year's figure as the change in prices, or earnings, between one year and the next.</li>
    <li><strong>Rounding.</strong> Each year's weekly amount is rounded to the nearest 5p, and the rounded amount is the base for the next year.</li>
    <li><strong>Buying power.</strong> The cash amount divided by a price index built from the prices figures. The starting year is 1.</li>
    <li><strong>Compared with earnings.</strong> The rise in the amount divided by the rise in an earnings index built from the earnings figures. It is an index, not a share of any pay figure in pounds.</li>
    <li><strong>Totals.</strong> 52 weeks a year at that year's rate.</li>
  </ul>
  <p><a href="/sources-and-method/#models">Full method, reference examples and limits</a>.</p>

  <script type="application/json" id="compare-data">{{ { scenarios: scenarios.scenarios, default: scenarios.default, default_years: scenarios.default_years, default_start: ("nsp-full-weekly" | figPence) | plainPounds, default_first: 2027 } | json | safe }}</script>
  <script type="module" src="/assets/js/tools/compare.js"></script>
</section>

<h2 id="social-care">Pensions and social care: the plan announced in September 2026</h2>

<p>On {{ ("pm-care-2026-09-29" | sourceRecord).published_date | timeTag | safe }} the Prime Minister announced two things together. One is a less generous rule for the yearly increase in the State Pension, from April 2030. The other is a National Care Service with free personal care for older people, using the savings. This section sets out what was announced, what is in law, what has only been reported, and which questions the plan leaves open.</p>

{% call m.note("The position on " ~ (news.as_of | timeTag), 3) %}
